<Layout title="About Pinak — Local by design" description="How Pinak works, what stays on your device, its open-source dependencies, and responsible personal archiving."><div class="wrap"><section class="page-intro"><span class="eyebrow">ABOUT / THE IDEA BEHIND THE COMMAND</span><h1>Local by design.<br/><span style="color:var(--accent)">Open by choice.</span></h1><p>A Python media toolkit that brings downloading, processing, and local playback into one terminal workflow.</p></section><article class="content-narrow" style="margin-left:0"><h2>What Pinak is</h2><p>Pinak is an educational, open-source wrapper around yt-dlp and FFmpeg for personal media archiving. It provides a CLI, an interactive shell, local download history, diagnostics, and a player for local audio, video, and pictures.</p><h2>Where the work happens</h2><p>When you use the installed package, your device connects directly to media sources. Pinak saves files to your selected folder and keeps settings and history locally. It does not operate a shared service that downloads files on your behalf.</p><p>Source websites, package repositories, and update/download services still receive the connections needed for those operations. “Local” does not mean that downloading happens offline or that outside platforms are absent.</p><h2>Connect through MCP</h2><p>Local MCP runs on your device through stdio. The optional Cloud edition runs on a server you operate, stores media there, and connects through authenticated Streamable HTTP. Pinak does not operate that server for you. Your AI provider may receive prompts and returned metadata in either mode.</p><p><a href={url('/mcp/')}>Compare Local and Cloud MCP →</a></p><h2>Built with excellent tools</h2><ul><li><a href="https://github.com/yt-dlp/yt-dlp">yt-dlp</a> — source extraction and downloading.</li><li><a href="https://ffmpeg.org/">FFmpeg</a> — merging, conversion, decoding, and processing.</li><li>Typer, Rich, and prompt-toolkit — command-line and interactive interfaces.</li><li>SQLite, platformdirs, and Pydantic Settings — local history and preferences.</li><li>Pillow, SoundDevice, NumPy, and Mutagen — media handling.</li></ul><h2 id="responsible-use">Personal archiving, responsibly</h2><p>Save media you own, that is freely licensed, or that you have explicit permission to keep. Respect creators and source-platform terms. Pinak does not bypass DRM, encryption, or access paywalls.</p><h2>Open source</h2><p>The package is released under the <a href={`${repository}/blob/main/LICENSE`}>MIT license</a>. Source version: <strong>{version}</strong>. This is not a claim about the latest published PyPI version.</p><p><a href={repository}>Browse the source ↗</a> · <a href={url('/docs/contributing/')}>Contribute →</a></p><h2>About this website</h2><p>This is a static informational site. It explains Pinak and presents demonstrations and supplied samples. It does not accept download URLs or upload visitor media. Theme and sound preferences may be saved in your browser; no analytics or advertising scripts are included.</p><p>The ambient sound is an original browser-synthesized composition, enabled only by your choice. Supplied screenshots and samples remain distinct from the project’s software license.</p></article></div></Layout>
